## The thing worth your attention

These converged on a gap the graph had no home for, so I seeded **`concepts/refusal-cost-symmetry`** — it reached `growing` (5 sources) within the same pass. The claim: an evaluation counting only the permissive failure measures conservatism, not safety, and a mechanism refusing everything tops it. It largely closes the standing false-refusal-cost open question in `permission-gate-as-architecture`.

It names a concrete change to **`/elevate`**. That skill's posture — "most cycles correctly produce zero proposals" — is structurally an aggressive prompt, and SoundnessBench measures what those do to research judgment: near-uniform rejection while the safety number improves. A quiet cycle and a gate that has started rejecting everything currently produce identical output. The fix is cheap (score known-good historical proposals alongside candidates), but it's your call — I recorded it rather than editing the skill.

Two papers also supply measured backing for choices this box already made on judgment: worktree isolation for parallel file-writing agents, and scoped rather than global rule loading.

